= Solution
{parent=a}

Use $\mathbb T^n=\mathbb R^n/(2\pi\mathbb Z)^n$ and the <periodic Sobolev space> convention
$$
\|f\|_{H^s}^2=\sum_{k\in\mathbb Z^n}(1+|k|^2)^s|\widehat f(k)|^2.
$$
The <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality> gives
$$
\sum_k|\widehat f(k)|\leq\|f\|_{H^s}\left(\sum_k(1+|k|^2)^{-s}\right)^{1/2}.
$$
To check the last sum, partition the lattice into $|k|<1$ and dyadic shells $2^j\leq|k|<2^{j+1}$. A shell contains at most $C_n2^{jn}$ points and contributes at most $C_n2^{j(n-2s)}$. This is a convergent <geometric series> exactly when $s>n/2$.

Thus the <Fourier series> $\sum_k\widehat f(k)e^{ik\cdot x}$ converges absolutely and uniformly on the <torus>. Its sum is a <continuous function> and has the original <Fourier coefficients>, so it represents the original periodic <distribution>. In particular the representative is unique and
$$
\boxed{H^s(\mathbb T^n)\hookrightarrow C(\mathbb T^n),\qquad\|f\|_\infty\leq C_{s,n}\|f\|_{H^s}\quad(s>n/2).}
$$
This proves the requested inclusion, with its continuous <Sobolev embedding> estimate.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional real or complex <inner product space>, and let $A\subseteq\operatorname{End}(V)$ be a unital <subalgebra> stable under the <adjoint operator>. Define its <commutant of an operator algebra> by $A'=\{T:Ta=aT\text{ for all }a\in A\}$. The <double commutant theorem for star-algebras> asserts $\boxed{A''=A}$. The inclusion $A\subseteq A''$ follows immediately from the definition; we prove the reverse.

Write $N=\dim V$, fix a <basis> $v_1,\ldots,v_N$, and act diagonally on $V^N$. The <linear subspace>
$$
W=\{(av_1,\ldots,av_N):a\in A\}
$$
is invariant under every diagonal $a$. Since $A$ is closed under adjoints, its <orthogonal complement> $W^\perp$ is invariant too: $\langle aw,z\rangle=\langle w,a^*z\rangle=0$ for $w\in W^\perp$, $z\in W$. Hence the <orthogonal projection> $P$ onto $W$ commutes with every diagonal $a$.

Writing $P$ as an $N$ by $N$ block <matrix> $(P_{ij})$, this says $P_{ij}a=aP_{ij}$, so each $P_{ij}\in A'$. If $b\in A''$, it commutes with every $P_{ij}$, and its diagonal action therefore commutes with $P$ and preserves $W$. Because $1\in A$, $(v_1,\ldots,v_N)\in W$. Therefore there is one $a\in A$ such that
$$
(bv_1,\ldots,bv_N)=(av_1,\ldots,av_N).
$$
Equality on the <basis> gives $b=a$, proving the theorem over either scalar field.

The identity hypothesis is necessary. For a possibly nonunital adjoint-stable <subalgebra>, adjoining the ambient identity does not change its <commutant of an operator algebra>, so the exact statement is $A''=A+\mathbb F I$. For example $A=\{0\}$ has $A''=\mathbb F I$ on a nonzero $V$. Thus the usual exam formulation takes the algebra to contain the identity.

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

At a fixed $t$, write $A=f(t)$ and $B=f'(t)$. Differentiating the <matrix exponential> in the direction $B$ gives
$$
\boxed{\frac{d}{dt}e^{f(t)}=\int_0^1e^{(1-u)f(t)}f'(t)e^{uf(t)}\,du.}
$$
The order of factors matters because <matrix multiplication> need not be commutative.

To prove the <derivative of the matrix exponential> formula, expand its <power series>. The coefficient linear in $h$ in $(A+hB)^k$ is $\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}A^jBA^{k-1-j}$. On bounded sets of <matrices>, the exponential series and its directional derivative series converge uniformly: the latter is dominated in <operator norm> by $\|B\|\sum_{k\geq1}k\|A\|^{k-1}/k!$. Terms with two or more $hB$ factors have sum $O(h^2)$ locally. Hence the derivative is
$$
\sum_{k\geq1}\frac1{k!}\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}A^jBA^{k-1-j}.
$$
On the other hand, the integral in the box expands absolutely as
$$
\sum_{a,b\geq0}\frac{A^aBA^b}{a!b!}\int_0^1(1-u)^au^b\,du
=\sum_{a,b\geq0}\frac{A^aBA^b}{(a+b+1)!}.
$$
The integral identity follows by repeated <integration by parts>. Grouping by $a+b=k-1$ gives the same derivative. Finally differentiability means $f(t+h)=A+hB+o(h)$; the locally bounded derivative of the exponential sends the $o(h)$ error to $o(h)$, proving the chain rule even when $f'$ is not continuous. If $[f,f']=0$, the formula simplifies to $e^ff'$.

